WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.120
Last time, we pooled all five
clinics into a single standard.

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:08.245
Drawn on the log-odds ruler,
that standard is one line:

00:00:08.285 --> 00:00:14.110
lower-risk patients start at 23%, and
higher risk is one step up, to 63%.

00:00:14.233 --> 00:00:16.006
Now put the clinics back.

00:00:16.046 --> 00:00:20.188
Alder's patients sit above the
line, at both ends. So do Dune's.

00:00:20.228 --> 00:00:23.566
Birch and Cedar sit below
it, at both ends. And Elm,

00:00:23.606 --> 00:00:27.852
with no readmissions at all,
is off the bottom of the chart.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.907
One line for everyone misses,
and it misses clinic by clinic.

00:00:31.947 --> 00:00:36.563
So here is today's question: what is
the smallest change that fixes it?

00:00:38.133 --> 00:00:40.354
Welcome back to Model in Motion.

00:00:40.394 --> 00:00:43.006
Today: one intercept per provider.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:49.685
The obvious fix is to give
every clinic a line of its own:

00:00:49.725 --> 00:00:52.567
its own starting point, and its own step.

00:00:52.607 --> 00:00:55.794
That's what we did for
Cedar, two episodes ago.

00:00:55.933 --> 00:00:57.621
But look at what it costs.

00:00:57.661 --> 00:01:01.003
Each line now rests on one
clinic's patients alone.

00:01:01.043 --> 00:01:04.042
Alder's higher-risk end is just 6 people.

00:01:04.082 --> 00:01:07.504
And the gap between two clinics
is no longer one number.

00:01:07.544 --> 00:01:12.902
Alder sits further above Cedar for
higher-risk patients than for lower-risk ones.

00:01:13.033 --> 00:01:16.906
So the question 'how much higher
is Alder?' would have two answers.

00:01:16.946 --> 00:01:20.274
With a dozen risk factors,
it would have thousands.

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.344
So let's make a bet instead.

00:01:22.384 --> 00:01:25.879
Suppose a risk factor works
the same way in every clinic:

00:01:25.919 --> 00:01:30.045
the same step on the log-odds
ruler, wherever you are treated.

00:01:30.085 --> 00:01:33.763
Then clinics can differ in
only one way: where they start.

00:01:36.233 --> 00:01:38.262
Here is that bet as a machine.

00:01:38.302 --> 00:01:43.922
One dial, beta, sets the step for everyone.
Turn it, and every line tilts together.

00:01:44.367 --> 00:01:46.940
And every clinic gets a slider of its own.

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:51.367
It moves that clinic's line up or
down, and leaves the tilt alone.

00:01:51.407 --> 00:01:53.293
We'll call that number gamma.

00:01:53.433 --> 00:01:57.169
Statisticians call a line's
starting point its intercept.

00:01:57.209 --> 00:02:02.252
So that's the whole idea: one intercept
per provider, and one shared step.

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:05.817
Written out: a patient's
log-odds is their clinic's gamma,

00:02:05.857 --> 00:02:08.163
plus beta if they are higher-risk.

00:02:08.203 --> 00:02:12.196
With more risk factors there are
more steps, each with its own beta.

00:02:12.236 --> 00:02:15.017
There is still just one gamma per clinic.

00:02:15.167 --> 00:02:18.007
This is the logistic fixed-effect model.

00:02:18.047 --> 00:02:21.780
'Fixed' means only that each
gamma is its own unknown number.

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:26.376
We assume nothing about how those
numbers are spread across clinics.

00:02:28.967 --> 00:02:34.369
How the dial and the sliders find their best
positions is a story for a later episode.

00:02:34.409 --> 00:02:36.848
For now, here is where they land.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.584
The shared step is 2.02.

00:02:39.624 --> 00:02:42.200
That is the bet at work: in every clinic,

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:47.249
higher risk multiplies the odds of
readmission by about seven and a half.

00:02:47.367 --> 00:02:51.424
Four sliders settle. Birch
is lowest, at minus 1.82.

00:02:51.464 --> 00:02:56.464
Then Cedar. Then Alder and
Dune, close together at the top.

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:59.504
Elm's never does. With no readmissions at all,

00:02:59.544 --> 00:03:03.664
a lower line always fits better,
so it slides off the chart.

00:03:03.704 --> 00:03:06.123
Elm gets an episode of its own.

00:03:06.267 --> 00:03:11.025
Switch to the probability ruler, and
the parallel lines become S-curves:

00:03:11.065 --> 00:03:13.011
one shape, slid sideways.

00:03:13.051 --> 00:03:18.707
For a lower-risk patient, the model
gives 14% at Birch and 34% at Alder.

00:03:18.747 --> 00:03:23.534
For a higher-risk patient,
55% at Birch and 80% at Alder.

00:03:23.667 --> 00:03:27.931
One caution. With 103 patients,
these are rough estimates.

00:03:27.971 --> 00:03:30.844
How rough is a question for later.

00:03:33.433 --> 00:03:41.378
Notice that the step has grown. The pooled
line climbed 1.75. These lines climb 2.02.

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:45.138
Here's why. Each clinic is
now compared only with itself:

00:03:45.178 --> 00:03:49.652
its own higher-risk patients
against its own lower-risk patients.

00:03:49.692 --> 00:03:59.173
Inside Alder, the step is 2.3. Inside
Birch, 1.78. Cedar, 1.79. Dune, 2.3.

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:01.597
Beta is a compromise among those four,

00:04:01.637 --> 00:04:05.361
weighted by how much each
clinic's patients can tell us.

00:04:05.401 --> 00:04:10.829
Elm has no say. All of its patients are
lower-risk, so it has no step to offer.

00:04:10.967 --> 00:04:16.068
Last time, the pooled rule mixed up how sick
patients are with where they were treated.

00:04:16.108 --> 00:04:19.247
Now the 'where' has a place
of its own: the gammas.

00:04:19.287 --> 00:04:21.281
That is the first thing we asked for:

00:04:21.321 --> 00:04:24.586
a risk rule learned from
patients who share a clinic.

00:04:27.167 --> 00:04:29.029
You might expect one more number:

00:04:29.069 --> 00:04:32.936
an overall starting point, with
each clinic measured from it.

00:04:33.067 --> 00:04:37.571
Try it. Raise that number by a half,
and lower every gamma by a half.

00:04:37.611 --> 00:04:42.269
Nothing on screen moves. The data cannot
tell those two descriptions apart,

00:04:42.309 --> 00:04:44.852
so the model keeps only the gammas.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:49.747
The same goes for anything that is
identical for every patient at a clinic:

00:04:49.787 --> 00:04:52.811
its size, its ownership, its neighbourhood.

00:04:52.851 --> 00:04:57.254
None of them can have a beta of its
own. They are already inside gamma.

00:04:58.833 --> 00:05:01.438
So what is a gamma? Read it carefully.

00:05:01.478 --> 00:05:05.637
Alder's is minus 0.65. That
is not a readmission rate.

00:05:05.677 --> 00:05:08.670
It is a position on the log-odds ruler:

00:05:08.710 --> 00:05:13.577
the log-odds for a patient with
no risk factor switched on.

00:05:13.617 --> 00:05:17.786
Here, that's a lower-risk
patient at Alder: 34%.

00:05:17.933 --> 00:05:21.502
With many risk factors, that
patient may not exist at all.

00:05:21.542 --> 00:05:26.496
So we will mostly read the gaps between
gammas, not the gammas themselves.

00:05:26.633 --> 00:05:29.302
And gamma is not a pure measure of care.

00:05:29.342 --> 00:05:34.790
It holds everything that is constant within
a clinic: how well it looks after patients,

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:38.763
but also anything about its
patients that we failed to measure.

00:05:38.803 --> 00:05:41.234
The data cannot separate the two.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:46.436
So read a higher gamma this way: for
patients who look the same on paper,

00:05:46.476 --> 00:05:50.627
more readmissions here than
there. Why is a separate question.

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:55.339
Does learning the rule
inside providers really help?

00:05:55.379 --> 00:06:01.489
Go back to our simulated crowd of 200
providers, where we set the truth ourselves.

00:06:01.529 --> 00:06:05.329
The true step for the main risk factor is 0.8.

00:06:05.467 --> 00:06:09.764
Pool everyone, and the estimate
is 0.71, because in this crowd,

00:06:09.804 --> 00:06:14.216
providers with sicker patients
happen to do a little better.

00:06:14.256 --> 00:06:19.150
Give every provider its own
gamma, and the estimate is 0.81.

00:06:20.733 --> 00:06:22.229
Let's gather it up.

00:06:22.269 --> 00:06:25.536
Every provider gets its own intercept: gamma.

00:06:25.576 --> 00:06:29.674
All providers share one step
for each risk factor: beta.

00:06:29.714 --> 00:06:33.670
And beta is learned from
comparisons inside providers.

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:37.856
Remember what this rests on: the
bet that the lines are parallel.

00:06:37.896 --> 00:06:41.512
A clinic that does well with
lower-risk patients and badly with

00:06:41.552 --> 00:06:44.935
higher-risk ones would have a
line that crosses the others.

00:06:44.975 --> 00:06:46.950
We will come back and check.

00:06:47.067 --> 00:06:51.166
Last time, we asked for two
things. The risk rule, we now have.

00:06:51.206 --> 00:06:56.392
And the yardstick? Line the gammas up, and
one of them sits in the middle: Cedar's.

00:06:56.432 --> 00:07:00.915
A middle clinic is a yardstick that no
clinic's size can pull toward itself.

00:07:00.955 --> 00:07:02.734
We'll build on it later.

00:07:02.867 --> 00:07:06.607
First, though: Alder's line
sits 0.87 above Cedar's.

00:07:06.647 --> 00:07:10.272
What does that gap mean for an actual patient?

00:07:10.312 --> 00:07:12.518
Next time: reading gamma.

00:07:12.667 --> 00:07:16.772
This has been Model in Motion.
Thanks for following the story.
